## Example 3: Handling an Empty Job List

The original `JobListings` component directly mapped through the jobs:

```js
{jobs.map((job) => (
  <JobListing key={job.id} job={job} />
))}
```

The problem was that if there were no jobs, the page would not give the user any information.

I improved it by checking the length of the jobs array:

```js
{jobs.length === 0 ? (
  <p>No jobs available at the moment.</p>
) : (
  jobs.map((job) => (
    <JobListing key={job.id} job={job} />
  ))
)}
```

### What I improved and learned

- I learned more about conditional rendering in React.
- The user now gets a message when there are no jobs.
- This makes the interface clearer instead of showing an empty section.

